  "guide": [
    {
      "title": "Main ___ raha / rahi hoon = I am ___ing",
      "body": "To say what's happening now: verb + raha/rahi + hoon. Men say raha, women say rahi — this little word shows who's speaking. \"Work\" is kaam kar (work do): Main kaam kar raha hoon. With aap, use rahe hain.",
      "examples": [
        "s-b9-1",
        "s-b9-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "___ boliye = Please say ___",
      "body": "Two polite tools. 1) Add -iye to a verb for \"please …\": boliye = please speak. 2) Kya aap … sakte hain? = Can you …? These are your safety net — when you're lost in a conversation, they keep it going.",
      "examples": [
        "s-b10-1",
        "s-b10-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Main ___ gaya / gayi = I went to ___",
      "body": "Going somewhere: Main bazaar gaya (man) / gayi (woman). Doing something to a thing (eat, drink, see): use maine, and the verb matches the THING, not you: Maine paani piya (water is masculine), Maine chai pi (tea is feminine) — same for men and women.",
      "examples": [
        "s-b11-1",
        "s-b11-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Main ___ jaunga / jaungi = I will go to ___",
      "body": "The future adds -ga (men) or -gi (women) to the end: jaunga / jaungi (will go), aaunga / aaungi (will come). For \"we\": hum … milenge (we will meet). Remember: kal + future = tomorrow.",
      "examples": [
        "s-b12-1",
        "s-b12-2"
      ]
    }
  ]
